{"id":"4362628","slug":"ja-marr-chase","name":"Ja'Marr Chase","position":"WR","team":"CIN","headshot":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/headshots/nfl/players/full/4362628.png","formats":{"ppr-12":{"positionRank":2,"ourRank":2,"vorRound":1,"adp":3.8,"gap":1.8,"projected":333.19,"vor":153.16,"expectedGames":15.36,"floor":245.97,"median":333.2,"ceiling":420.43,"tier":1,"tierDrop":23.4}},"capsule":{"body":"Chase scored under the position's weekly median exactly once last season, a 4.6 in week 1, then cleared 15 points in 11 of his 16 weeks. His best week was a 38.1 in week 7. That is the shape of a receiver you never think about on Sunday, and it matters more than the year-over-year dip everyone will bring up at the draft.\n\nThat dip is real: 23.7 points a game in 2024 down to 19.6 in 2025, a full WR1 season becoming WR4. But the targets went the other way, from 27.9% to 32.3%, and he played every week but one suspension game across two seasons. Our model pays him for that: +10.1 a game from the target share, another +8 from a 94.4% snap share, and the projection lands at 333 points, WR2 on our board. He is one player left in his tier before a 23.4-point cliff, so the gap between him and the next guy is the largest on the board.\n\nThe quibble inside the season is scoring range. He drew 22 red-zone touches, WR4, but only 11.7% of his own touches came there, WR106, and his average target traveled just 8.5 yards. He is converting volume, not eating deep touchdowns, which is also why 19.6 a game felt lighter than 1412 yards and 128 touches, WR2 and one spot behind Puka Nacua, should. With 15 expected games and Zac Taylor still calling plays, we would take him at his 3.8 ADP every time and never look back.","pulls":[],"writtenAt":"2026-10-13T21:57:56.516Z"},"weekRead":null,"season":{"season":2026,"format":"ppr-12","weeks":18,"through":5,"games":5,"pointsPerGame":16,"totals":{"pass_attempts":0,"completions":0,"passing_yards":0,"passing_touchdowns":0,"interceptions":0,"carries":0,"ru